A||||10||Phoenix||Iron Duck Owners - Oil consumption question||||5||
Z||000000||Phoenix||07-14-2000||08:55 AM||||I have an 88 Duck with no oil leaks/drips, it doesn't smoke at startup or running, doesn't smoke durring deceleration (sucking oil through valve guide seals) but uses about a quart of oil/1000 miles. The engine has 55,000 mile on it and runs great, I've been using 20-50 castrol syntec. Anyone else experiance this kind of oil consumption? I know some engines are a bit worse than others  so this could be case of loose tolerances.<P>TIA ||||reg||5
Z||000001||lennyb4||07-14-2000||09:07 AM||||First off thats some really heavy oil. I use 10W-30 in my duck and dont go through any oil. I have 98,000 miles on her and climbing. If your going through a quart every 1,000 miles its gotta be going somewhere. As for being loose...I dont think so with only 55,000 miles on it. I would look for leaks somewhere, and take a peak at your anti-freeze for oil.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phoenix||07-14-2000||09:15 AM||||Just flushed the cooling system a week ago, it was "Green & Clean".  Additionally, When I bought the car it had been sitting for several years.  Maybe some chevron upper cylinder cleaner would help if the oil control rings a gummed up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lennyb4||07-14-2000||09:19 AM||||7 year old oil...Now thats gotta be nasty. I would run a cleaner through it and give it a real good oil change and see if that helps. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phoenix||07-14-2000||09:30 AM||||Oils not that old - I got the car last March and changed the oil right away. I have changed it twice since and driven the car about 4500 miles. Ya - 7 year old oil would be nasty! but not the case here.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||07-14-2000||09:41 AM||||I wouldn't worry, Phoenix.  My 88 Coupe uses a quart every 1200 miles or so.  It did that when I bought it at 66K miles and it still does it at 112K.  It hasn't gotten any worse, and the car runs great, so I don't worry about it.  (I use 5W-30 Valvoline dino oil, if it matters.)<P>And Lenny, you must have the only oil-tight Iron Duke Pontiac ever made!||||reg||1
Z||000006||lennyb4||07-14-2000||09:53 AM||||I do have a very small leak. On the valve cover. The person i bought the car from had all the gasgets replaced with rubber about two years ago. Also im kinda a stickler for leaks, i cant stand them (oil, water, what ever). If one happens to pop up then i try to fix it ASAP.<p>[This message has been edited by lennyb4 (edited 07-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||07-14-2000||04:48 PM||||My dads '87 Duke uses a quart of oil about every 1500 miles... its a remanufactured engine with about 28,000 miles on it, but has done this since new.  I recall the original engine regularly consumed oil, but I am not sure how much.  No smoking, no leaks.  I wouldn't worry too much, so long as your not getting oil coming out your exhaust pipes.||||reg||1
Z||000008||TheFiero64||07-14-2000||04:59 PM||||My 84 SE uses a quart every 1500 or so. It has 120,000 on it so I don't worry about it. I do need to replace the valve cover gasket though I know that leaks. I wouldn't worry. I rather use some between oil changes than none. It lets you know that everything is getting lubricated. I also use syntec but I use 5w-50 It is better for startups<P>------------------<BR>Joshua Bumpus<BR>New Bedford, MA<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fi.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000009||Sage||07-14-2000||05:18 PM||||Rebuilt the 2.5 in my wifes 84(87block)about 10,000mi. ago.  Changed @3,000mi. intervals, and it seems to use about 1/2qt. in between changes.  Can see evidence of very slight leak around pcv and the breather that the air cleaner plugs into.  Didn't replace the rubber in either place and it does have a tendency to harden and allow passage at high rpms(highway speeds).  <P>The other obvious "leak" is the valve cover gasket.  I don't think they make one that doesn't leak.  Haven't tried the black rtv there, but that would probably seal it up.<P>A quart every 1,000...I wouldn't be too concerned.  If the car sat for any extended period of time, it might have worn oil rings, and/or slightly worn or scored cyl. walls, as a result of scraping away rust/corrosion build up on initial start up. <P>If you are not getting oil in your coolant, or versa visa, I would just enjoy driving it, and add as needed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Just my .002...Have a good one!||||reg||1
Z||000010||ur2biteme||07-20-2000||09:06 AM||||AM I THE ONLY PERSON WHO HAS A DUKE THAT DOESN'T USE OIL??????  mine leaks, i should say POURS oil on the ground, but doesnt use any.  have to add a quart every 1000-1200 miles with 165k on the original '87 DIS engine.  it also has always seemed to pull harder than other duke fieros i've driven as well.  <P>if it's using oil but not showins smoke, i wouldnt worry too much.  as soon as you see blue smoke or your oil presure starts doing wacky things, then worry.  <P>also, from what i've been told, synthetic oils have a tendency to make oil consumption/leaking problems worse.  the oil seems to be thinner than the natural stuff of the same weight.  my friend has an 82 and 81 280zx, both approching 200k, the 82 was running synthetic when he bought it and consumed a lot of oil, slight blue smoke.  changed to the same weight regular oil (10w40 i think) and the consumption dropped and the smoke dissappeared.  <P>f.y.i.  i use quaker state 10w40.  the stuff that advertises on the bottle "protects over 3000 miles".  i change it around 3500 anyway.  personal preference, you don't have to take my advise, i'd steer clear of the synthetic oil.  the other synthetic fluids are great, but i'd go with regular oil.....||||reg||1
